WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
COHEN: Good morning, everyone.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:07.070
For those of you who can't see me, my name
is Abby Joseph Cohen.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:14.280
I'm the senior investment strategist at Goldman
Sachs, and a very proud member of the Council

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.750
on Foreign Relations.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:23.570
I have the honor today of introducing Dr.
Olivier Blanchard, who is the chief economist

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.260
of the International Monetary Fund.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:32.050
This is such an important time for the global
economy as it has been for the last several

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.180
years.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:41.610
And Mr. Blanchard has been at the IMF in very
important responsibilities during this period.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.610
He has a very distinguished academic career.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.379
I know you all have a copy of his biography.

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:54.890
But he has spent much of his academic career
at MIT, where he has headed the economics

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:55.890
department.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.239
He's also taught at Harvard.

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:05.280
And he has written a textbook with the incoming
vice chair of the Federal Reserve, Stanley

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.280
Fischer.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:14.049
So without further ado, I would like to invite
Mr. Blanchard, one of my favorite macroeconomists,

00:01:14.049 --> 00:01:26.590
up to the podium so that we can begin our
discussion.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:38.170
BLANCHARD: Thank you for inviting me.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:47.689
What I thought I would do is to give you a
10-minute summary of the update that I gave

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:49.380
two days ago.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.090
Four times a year, the fund is supposed to
indicate how it sees the world.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.570
And I had to do this in snowy Washington two
days ago.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:05.729
I emphasized three themes.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:08.770
The first one is the recovery's strengthening.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.800
I was hoping this would make headlines.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:18.840
And the two numbers -- I'm not going to inundate
you with numbers -- but the two numbers there

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.610
that world growth 2013 was 3 percent.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:29.150
And we predict -- we forecast it to be about
3.7 percent in 2014.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.360
That's a non-negligible improvement, although
it's still far below the more than 4 percent

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.090
that we used to have.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:44.420
The second is that actually we -- this is
largely -- this is a recovery which was largely

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.319
forecasted in the sense that our forecast
revisions are very, very small.

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:54.480
We just revised our number for 2014 by 0.1
percent.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.760
Therefore, what happened is very much what
we expected to happen.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.739
Doesn't always happen that way, but it did.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:08.330
And then the third one is heterogeneity, which
is that it is not the case that all countries

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:09.790
are recovering at the same pace.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.670
There are fairly major differences across
the world.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:16.440
And it's not only important to know, but they
interact with each other so that we're not

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:23.950
out of the woods, and that there are some
downside risks which strike us as rather serious.

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.260
So these are the headlines.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:32.680
And the question is, well, why is the world
economy recovering?

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:39.120
I found it useful for the past few years to
think of forces pulling the economy forward,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.080
and then forces pushing it back, keeping it
in place.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.310
So you can think of -- I've used this analogy
of accelerator on one hand, and brakes on

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:50.310
the other.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.260
I think it's a useful way of thinking about
it.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:59.930
So on the accelerator side, I mean, it's clear
that there has been an incredibly strong accelerator

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:08.260
now for many years, which is accommodative
monetary policy, just getting interest rates

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:14.400
to zero, or very close to, was a fairly strong
monetary expansion.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:21.239
I think we can discuss how much the unconventional
monetary policy, which was pursued thereafter,

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:23.070
how big the effect was.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.960
I think it was not very large.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.110
It was probably worth doing.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:33.030
But the point is that this has been an incredibly
accommodative monetary policy, which is continuing.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:40.530
And if you put yourself in, say, 2006, and
I tell you the major central banks are going

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:46.190
to keep interest rates at zero for the next
three or four years, you would have said,

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:50.350
"Well, it is going to lead to overheating
on a scale we've never seen that's going to

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.740
lead to inflation or more."

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.660
But that's not what we have seen.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.310
So the accelerator is there.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.690
But clearly, it hasn't worked, because -- because
of the brakes.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:06.139
So it's important to identify the brakes,
and what we argue is that the brakes are slowly

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:14.280
loosening in various parts of the world, so
that the accelerator is getting force.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.300
My list of brakes, would have three main ones.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.970
The first one is fiscal consolidation.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:28.160
As you well know, the crisis itself, the decrease
in output led to a very large increase in

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.220
public debt.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.410
And something had to be done about it.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.010
So fiscal consolidation was needed.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:40.520
There was the hope by some -- and this was
very much major controversy in, say, 2011,

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.720
2012 -- that this fiscal consolidation would
not affect demand too much, would actually

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:52.139
boost morale, and not stand in the way of
a recovery.

00:05:52.139 --> 00:05:57.290
There was this notion that if you had really
strong fiscal consolidation, then you would

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:02.740
instill confidence in people, and therefore,
they would spend more, so that the second

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.060
effect would dominate the first.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:07.580
It think this has been thoroughly debunked.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:13.120
The effect of fiscal consolidation is to decrease
demand, and to decrease growth in the short

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:14.120
run.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.800
So here, I'm not saying that should not have
been done.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.750
It had to be done.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.800
But it clearly slowed down the recovery by
quite a bit.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.289
There's no question, for example, for the
U.S., that last year, fiscal consolidation

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:31.680
played a really important role in leading
to a relatively small growth -- growth number.

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:32.850
So that's the first.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:39.970
The second is the health of the financial
system, as translated in the credit conditions

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:47.710
that you and I, you know, households and firms,
can get when they borrow.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.520
And that in turn depends on the health of
the banks, and depends on the health of the

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.500
borrowers themselves.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:59.190
And if there's a large spread between the
policy rate, the rate that monetary policy

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:05.380
sets, and the rates at which we can borrow,
then again, that's a fairly major brake on

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.160
demand on the recovery.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.900
And then the third is fuzzier, but I think
very relevant.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:19.220
And in technical terms, we call it "Knightian
uncertainty" for Frank Knight.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:24.169
And this is this notion that there's uncertainty,
but not of the usual kind; not the uncertainty

00:07:24.169 --> 00:07:28.340
where you say there's probably 0.3 that this
will happen, and probably 0.7 that this will

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:33.730
happen, but more the world in which we say,
"I don't understand," which is a different

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.199
world from the first one.

00:07:35.199 --> 00:07:40.569
And it's clear that this played a very major
role early in the crisis, where what was happening

00:07:40.569 --> 00:07:46.680
had never happened, and we couldn't just think
about what was -- I think that, again, that

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.500
has played a major role.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:53.610
What has happened to each of these brakes,
I think, looking into the recent past, and

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.800
looking to the future, is they're loosening.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:01.509
Fiscal consolidation has been not completely
done, but the worst of it has been done in

00:08:01.509 --> 00:08:04.070
most countries.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:07.340
Banks are getting back to health at various
rates.

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:14.310
I think the risks for banks are more in, what
I call, the "second moments," the risk.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:18.030
But they are still lending at relatively low
rates in many countries.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:22.710
And I think that type of Knightian uncertainty
is also disappearing.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:27.700
We're dealing with problems which are more
of a type that we have seen in the past.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.009
So that's the general frame.

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:35.690
And then let me use this to go very quickly
around the world, and tell you where I think

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.320
the various countries are.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:44.000
So for the U.S., my sense is the accelerator
is most definitely still there.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.080
And the brakes are nearly entirely gone; which
is fiscal consolidation, which was strong

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:54.510
last year, is going to still be here this
year, but much less.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:57.130
I think we've been lucky this time.

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:59.930
Banks -- you know, no bank is in perfect shape.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:06.540
But banks are lending at low rates so that
people want to get a mortgage or a business

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.260
loan, can get it.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:15.779
So my sense is that the recovery is very strong,
is based on the very wide set of factors.

00:09:15.779 --> 00:09:16.779
Private demand is strong.

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:17.779
It was strong last year.

00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:20.310
I mean, last year, private demand was 4 percent.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.620
Now, growth was only 2, because of the fiscal
consolidation.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.480
But it was already strong.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.320
I think it's going to get stronger.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:30.380
So I think the U.S. is really in a solid recovery.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:34.810
And I don't have much worry about it.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:40.529
I think that we now can start talking about
an upside risk, which is that -- you know,

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:45.920
think again of the accelerator being on its
own, and the brakes being gone.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.310
It could be that demand is actually stronger
than we currently expect.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:54.000
This would -- this would be handled -- this
would be handled by the Fed, which would have

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.330
to increase interest rates faster than currently
intends to.

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.730
But that would create a lot of disruptions
in financial markets.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:04.620
It's not priced in and would complicate it.

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.160
Next, I'm trying to order things.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:12.550
So the next group of countries where things
are clearly better -- that maybe is going

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:19.990
to sound paradoxical -- I would put the U.K.
and the Euro Core, both Germany and France.

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:25.610
I think if you look at those countries, again,
the accelerator is there, both from the Bank

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:28.590
of England and the ECB.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:33.820
Fiscal consolidation, which was very strong
in the U.K., has been turned down quite a

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:35.040
bit.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.240
Fiscal consolidation in Germany is non-existent.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.390
They didn't need to consolidate, so they're
not doing it.

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:44.540
And France, which had a lot of fiscal consolidation,
is doing much better this year.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:47.110
Banks -- again, banks are not in perfect shape.

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:49.420
But in all these countries, they are lending
at low rates.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:51.260
Their spreads are small.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:55.089
And so my sense is that although the numbers
are not incredibly exciting yet -- they're

00:10:55.089 --> 00:10:59.910
not in the U.S. range -- there is no reason
to think that the recovery will not continue

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.339
in those countries.

00:11:02.339 --> 00:11:04.589
Continuing, this is the tour.

00:11:04.589 --> 00:11:06.680
This is if it's Tuesday, it's Belgium.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:10.660
The usual, very quick tour of the world.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:12.310
Japan.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:17.750
So Japan, the growth numbers are better than
I think anything we had expected two years

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:18.750
ago.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:26.190
We have about -- about 1.7 percent I think
for 2013, 1.7 percent for 2014.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.920
These are good numbers.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:30.890
Is the recovery on solid ground?

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:35.430
I think there's still question mark in the
sense that if you look at where the growth

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:42.560
is coming from, it's coming largely from the
fiscal stimulus last year, and largely from

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:48.140
exports; some of it, itself, due to the depreciation
of the yen.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:54.339
For this to continue, then, you know, it has
to move to consumption, which it has to a

00:11:54.339 --> 00:11:55.339
very limited extent.

00:11:55.339 --> 00:11:57.840
It has to move to investment, which it has
not.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:59.990
So there, it could happen.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:04.380
When you have growth, then consumers eventually
spend more, firms spend more.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:05.870
But we haven't seen the next step.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.480
And that's a bit worrisome.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:09.680
Two more remarks on Japan.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:16.230
You know, whether they reach for 2 percent
inflation that they have committed to is,

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:19.060
from an economic point of view, more or less
irrelevant.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.570
Japan will not be different, whether it's
1.5 or whether it's 2.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.029
Symbolically, it is important.

00:12:24.029 --> 00:12:28.589
They have put this as a goal, but it's not
going to make a whole lot of difference.

00:12:28.589 --> 00:12:32.410
What counts is output growth, and that comes
from many other things, not just having 2

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:33.769
percent inflation.

00:12:33.769 --> 00:12:36.830
And then the point for Japan is really not
growth now.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:43.270
It's this incredibly difficult balancing act
of the speed of fiscal consolidation.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:51.880
And as you all know, they have extremely high
public debt, in net terms, around 160 percent

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:52.880
of GDP.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.450
So they have to do something about it.

00:12:54.450 --> 00:13:01.610
But if they do it -- if they don't do it,
then credibility is in play, then, you know,

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:05.529
the rate on Japanese sovereign bonds will
go up.

00:13:05.529 --> 00:13:11.890
And when you have 160 percent, and you have
to pay, say, 300 basis points more on each,

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:14.149
it becomes rather expensive and difficult.

00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:15.149
So they have to avoid that.

00:13:15.149 --> 00:13:20.750
At the same time, if they do too much, then
they kill growth in the short run, and then

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:21.750
things are not better.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:25.110
So they have this really delicate balance,
which is, I think, going to be the issue for

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:33.339
Japan for -- for many years to come.

00:13:33.339 --> 00:13:38.730
My text has appeared under me, so I have to
go back to it.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:40.470
Three more.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:50.420
I suspect I've exhausted my time probably.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.440
Southern Europe.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:57.680
And to me, this is still the place where there
is good reasons to worry.

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:00.180
The picture is mixed.

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:03.589
On the one hand, they are doing better on
the external side.

00:14:03.589 --> 00:14:07.140
They have -- as you know, many of these countries
started with large current account deficits,

00:14:07.140 --> 00:14:10.029
so they had to do something to with competitiveness.

00:14:10.029 --> 00:14:14.420
And exports are going faster than the markets
are in.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:16.630
So the market share is increasing.

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:17.810
That's good news.

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:23.269
The bad news is internal demand, which is
weak in some cases, actually going down.

00:14:23.269 --> 00:14:27.820
And that's the result of a number of years
now of very weak activity, which has led to

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:36.050
weak firms, which has led to weak banks, which
forces more fiscal consolidation.

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:41.610
And there, we -- another analogy away from
accelerator and brakes, but adverse loops

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:46.690
are very powerful; which is if you have a
weak economy, then firms are weaker, banks

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:50.151
are weaker, they lend less, and so on, and
so on.

00:14:50.151 --> 00:14:53.620
And I think there are still reasons to worry
there.

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:56.240
They haven't turned the corner.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:00.839
I think this is going to be an issue for some
time.

00:15:00.839 --> 00:15:05.160
Last, emerging markets.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:09.019
So I think in general, we're fairly optimistic.

00:15:09.019 --> 00:15:15.920
We have emerging markets growing at -- as
a whole, at 6 percent.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:19.430
It seems to me that it's largely business
as usual.

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:24.199
And there are some countries which are overheating,
some countries which have shortfalls in demand,

00:15:24.199 --> 00:15:26.990
some countries which have structural problems,
some not.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:29.020
Brazil doesn't have enough investment.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.100
India has administrative problems, which stand
in the way.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:38.620
I think the big question probably from an
internal point of view is China, and whether

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:44.959
they're able to control the credit growth
at the rate at which they want; neither too

00:15:44.959 --> 00:15:46.230
fast, nor too slow.

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.410
And this is not a trivial task for them.

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:54.850
In terms of the external environment that
they face, they're clearly going to face two

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:57.209
forces going in opposite direction.

00:15:57.209 --> 00:16:01.519
The first one is to the extent that the U.S.
and other advanced economies are strengthening.

00:16:01.519 --> 00:16:06.910
This will increase their exports to various
degrees, depending on how open they are, and

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:08.540
what they sell.

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:12.670
But it's clear that interest rates, financial
conditions, are going to become tighter.

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:16.570
And they are going to be facing capital outflows
for some time.

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:22.610
And there was a fairly large amount of a capital
inflows we saw over the last -- over the last

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:26.820
few years, which went there, because it was
unattractive in the U.S. When it becomes more

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.019
attractive in the U.S., it will go back.

00:16:29.019 --> 00:16:31.529
I think we can expect this to happen.

00:16:31.529 --> 00:16:36.600
The evidence is that it's going to happen
mostly from countries which are not in great

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.440
shape from a macro point of view.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:43.029
So if your house is not in order, not only
do you have to repair it, but you have to

00:16:43.029 --> 00:16:46.399
be ready for capital outflows, and that's
a challenge that some countries are going

00:16:46.399 --> 00:16:48.290
to face.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:58.370
In general, what the work we have done at
the Fund suggests that on net, the fact that

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:02.139
stronger growth in the U.S. is going to come
with high interest rates, is on net going

00:17:02.139 --> 00:17:06.839
to be a good thing for emerging markets, that
they'll get more from the increase in exports,

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:10.850
than they'll be affected adversely by the
increasing interest rates.

00:17:10.850 --> 00:17:15.299
On net tapering rate happens -- and it will
happen in response to the U.S. economy doing

00:17:15.299 --> 00:17:17.160
better -- is probably a good thing.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:25.929
But there's going to be a lot of controversies
about this for some time to come.

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:33.470
Just wondering about two more points, two
risks we should be worried about.

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:38.780
So let me indicate one risk I'm not terribly
worried about is tapering.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:45.600
It seems to me that the exit from unconventional
monetary policy is conceptually not terribly

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:51.520
difficult to achieve; technically, not extremely
difficult to achieve.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:52.710
But it has never been done.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:57.080
We don't know exactly what the effect of measures
on rates are.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:02.549
And so communication issues are going to be
very substantial, so that I expect -- episodes

00:18:02.549 --> 00:18:07.220
maybe not of the magnitude we saw last May,
but something like this to happen again and

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:08.220
again.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.549
I think -- I tend to think of this as bumps
in the road.

00:18:10.549 --> 00:18:13.919
I don't see this as a systemic crisis coming
back.

00:18:13.919 --> 00:18:18.170
But I suspect it's going to be a bumpy path,
but not more than that.

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:21.480
The other is euro deflation.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:24.770
So again, in our baseline, we don't have deflation.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:30.450
I think that's the only place in the world
in this stage where I worry about deflation.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:35.300
We do not predict deflation, but we see this
as a risk.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:43.700
And our models give us about a 10 percent
probability of deflation in the Eurozone this

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:44.840
year.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:49.490
The swap, inflation swaps, euro inflation
swaps, give roughly the same number.

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:51.330
Why does it matter?

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:56.520
W Well, deflation is never very good, but
there is a particular aspect, which is important

00:18:56.520 --> 00:19:02.700
for Europe, which is that countries in the
south have to become more competitive relative

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:03.940
to countries in the north.

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:08.300
And in a fixed exchange rate, the only way
you can do this is by having less inflation

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:09.789
than the average.

00:19:09.789 --> 00:19:12.090
That's the only way you can actually become
more competitive.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:18.290
Now, if the Eurozone has -- just take a number
-- 2 percent inflation, then you can have

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:23.190
zero inflation, and you'll improve your competitiveness
by 2 percent a year.

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:28.770
If the Eurozone has zero percent inflation,
then you have to have deflation yourself.

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:30.560
And deflation is a very costly thing.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:35.059
It makes the real interest rates adjusted
for inflation higher, decreases real value

00:19:35.059 --> 00:19:36.480
of the debt.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:43.539
It probably has adverse income distribution
effects, so that the -- if there is deflation

00:19:43.539 --> 00:19:48.330
on average in the Eurozone, then the adjustment
process, which I talked about for Southern

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:50.179
Europe, may be derailed.

00:19:50.179 --> 00:19:53.230
And I think that's a very serious issue.

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:54.800
So that's a risk I worry about.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:59.800
Let me stop here and take questions.

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:01.460
Good.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:11.380
COHEN: (OFF-MIKE) force around the world,
giving us a sense of the likely outcomes,

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:14.500
but also very importantly, the potential risks.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:19.309
And I'd like to address one of the risks that
you touched on very briefly.

00:20:19.309 --> 00:20:27.630
And that is in the world's second-largest
economy, China, there are signs perhaps of

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:28.950
a deceleration.

00:20:28.950 --> 00:20:31.120
We see that in manufacturing.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:35.730
You mentioned concerns about this liquidity
adjustment there as well.

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:41.169
But if you could spend just a moment giving
us your thoughts in terms of the bigger changes

00:20:41.169 --> 00:20:48.130
in China, that for the last two decades, the
Chinese government has pursued economic policies

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:56.630
focused on liquidity, heavy spending on infrastructure,
and of course, exports, benefiting from low

00:20:56.630 --> 00:20:58.659
labor costs.

00:20:58.659 --> 00:21:05.539
What is clear I think to many is that the
new leadership in China is looking in terms

00:21:05.539 --> 00:21:12.260
of the new economic model; one in which they
need to rein in the liquidity, and also one

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:18.750
in which they build up domestic demand, rather
than just export demand, recognizing, for

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:24.809
example, that labor costs in China are perhaps
no longer the lowest in the region.

00:21:24.809 --> 00:21:29.630
If you can give us some sense of what the
path might be.

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:35.230
You know, obviously, China, and other emerging
markets, bounced back pretty quickly from

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:38.810
the so-called taper tantrum last spring.

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:44.350
But there are nevertheless these longer-term
concerns about the adjustment in the Chinese

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:46.820
economic model.

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:49.299
BLANCHARD: Good.

00:21:49.299 --> 00:21:53.780
Yes, I do not consider myself a specialist
of China.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:56.770
But I try to follow what's going on there.

00:21:56.770 --> 00:22:01.909
I would make four remarks.

00:22:01.909 --> 00:22:05.900
The first one is in the short run, I think
the issue is how do they handle the growth

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:09.250
in shadow banking, and in social financing?

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:15.680
And the question is how much control do they
have on it, and what tools do they want to

00:22:15.680 --> 00:22:16.680
use?

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:22.740
For example, do they let some local authorities
go bankrupt, taking the risk that this might

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:26.260
create contagion and have other effects.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:33.610
How much control do they have on local governments
in telling them not to invest in this or that?

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:36.080
I think that's what they're confronted with.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:41.919
And the sense is that they're making some
progress, but they don't have complete control

00:22:41.919 --> 00:22:42.919
of the issue.

00:22:42.919 --> 00:22:44.710
So it could go one way or the other.

00:22:44.710 --> 00:22:49.160
They could be unable to control it, and then,
you know, we have more of a credit boom, and

00:22:49.160 --> 00:22:50.850
then we know how this ends.

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:58.750
Are they controlling too well, and they get
recession by Chinese standards, which is probably

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:03.420
still fairly large positive growth rate, but
less than what we have.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:08.929
Again, our forecast for China is 7.5 percent,
which is optimistic, but I think has a fairly

00:23:08.929 --> 00:23:12.240
large distribution around it.

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:18.809
The second is on how serious the reformers
are.

00:23:18.809 --> 00:23:21.010
And I think there, there's no question that
they are serious.

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:26.180
I think that everything we hear is that they
really think the current model based on very

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:29.550
high investment rates is coming to an end,
that they cannot continue.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.850
They have to move away from it.

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:34.660
So I think that they are convinced that they
need to do something.

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:37.500
I think they have a sense of what they want
to do.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:43.060
And again, this is completely outside of my
sphere of knowledge.

00:23:43.060 --> 00:23:48.320
But people who look at the policy aspects,
seeing that they have the power to do it,

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:54.880
or at least more power to do it than any of
their predecessors for a long time.

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:58.700
The last point I would make is, it's clear
what has to happen.

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:03.260
Initially, we said they have to decrease the
current account surplus, move from external

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:08.530
demand to internal demand, and also move from
investment to consumption.

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:13.350
The current account surplus has decreased
quite a bit, I think -- we think largely for

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:15.750
structural reasons.

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:20.520
But the shift from investment to consumption
hasn't taken place yet.

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:28.360
It has to take place, I think, for social
unrest, social considerations, political considerations.

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:33.980
And if you look at the reforms that the third
plan had suggested of the earlier five-year

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:39.230
plan -- had suggested it looks as if it should
help go in that direction.

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.789
That's the assessment I have.

00:24:41.789 --> 00:24:42.820
COHEN: OK.

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:45.230
Let's turn our attention to Europe.

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:51.789
Clearly, you expressed your concern that one
of the potential risks would be deflation

00:24:51.789 --> 00:24:52.789
in Europe.

00:24:52.789 --> 00:24:59.400
So let's drill down a little bit more in terms
of the actual growth and inflation expectations.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.810
You indicated that core Europe is starting
to do better.

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:09.090
But even there, there's a very wide range,
the U.K. growing perhaps 2.4 percent, but

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.410
France, still under 1 percent.

00:25:11.410 --> 00:25:18.400
What are the potential ways in which France
and some of the other nations can bolster

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:19.929
their domestic demand?

00:25:19.929 --> 00:25:26.500
How do they address their labor problems in
terms of persistent high unemployment rates,

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:29.049
and so on?

00:25:29.049 --> 00:25:34.679
BLANCHARD: So I think it's a case-by-case
thing.

00:25:34.679 --> 00:25:42.529
For the U.K, one has a sense that the momentum
is there, so it's not obvious that they need

00:25:42.529 --> 00:25:43.529
to do much more.

00:25:43.529 --> 00:25:44.529
They still have an output gap.

00:25:44.529 --> 00:25:46.169
They still have high unemployment.

00:25:46.169 --> 00:25:51.610
But unemployment, as you know, is decreasing
very fast, and because productivity growth

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:56.520
is so dismal, so that any increase in output
leads to a large decrease in unemployment.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:26:01.690
So I think for the U.K., the question is why
is productivity growth so low?

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:04.490
We've done some work on it.

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:08.470
Much of it comes from low productivity growth
in services, and not, terribly surprisingly,

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:15.140
from productivity growth or productivity negative
growth in the financial sector.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:21.400
So, you know, I think this is part of reorganization
of the financial sector.

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:26.289
What exactly needs to be done there I think
is for people like you to say, rather than

00:26:26.289 --> 00:26:27.289
for people like me.

00:26:27.289 --> 00:26:33.720
But I think that in terms of demand, should
they do much more?

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:34.720
They have been very reluctant.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:35.980
And that's true of many countries.

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:39.850
They have been very reluctant to do public
investment.

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:47.980
And it strikes us as -- not only in the U.K.,
but elsewhere -- as a good time to do public

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:48.980
investment.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:53.570
Governments, states, can borrow at very, very
low rates.

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:58.000
And this is an environment in which a private
firm would think it's a good time to actually

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:01.530
bring forward some of the projects, and start
them now, rather than later.

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:04.270
COHEN: Isn't that also true in the United
States right now?

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:07.330
BLANCHARD: This is very much true of the United
States right now.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:09.940
This is true of many, many countries.

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:15.200
Fiscal consolidation has partly taken the
form of a decrease in public investment.

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:19.950
We know it's unwise, but we know it's unavoidable,
because of the politics of it.

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:21.799
That's the easiest thing to cut.

00:27:21.799 --> 00:27:26.419
And in many countries, we think public investment
is too low.

00:27:26.419 --> 00:27:32.470
Turning to Germany, things are going reasonably
well.

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:37.610
But again, what is striking is how -- and
the current account surplus of Germany comes

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:45.390
largely from more or less a normal level of
saving, but very low investment, both private,

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:47.420
but even more so, public.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:51.670
And there, I think that's another place where
there must be some highways which are not

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:56.299
in perfect shape, and they could do more on
the public-investment side.

00:27:56.299 --> 00:28:01.010
This would be good for the short run, be good
for the long run.

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:03.169
For France, I think the issue is a bit different.

00:28:03.169 --> 00:28:09.190
I think there has been -- if you look at France,
objectively, you look at the state of banks,

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:13.409
you look at the degree of competitiveness.

00:28:13.409 --> 00:28:16.740
It's not perfect, but the current account
deficit is relatively small.

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:18.280
We should worry about it.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:20.100
It's not a very big issue.

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:26.450
What's very striking about France is low level
of confidence of both households, which affects

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:30.200
consumption, and firms, which affects investment.

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:32.990
Where does this come from?

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:34.570
People say that there's a French dummy (ph).

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:39.799
A French fixed effect that the French are
always worried about the future -- I don't

00:28:39.799 --> 00:28:46.690
know if it's true -- but basically, something
having to do with a lack of clarity of the

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:54.330
policy, which has been in place now for a
year.

00:28:54.330 --> 00:29:01.740
There one can hope that -- you know, there
was a speech by the prime minister last week,

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:09.260
indicating a change of direction, and an attempt
to limit or decrease the level of spending,

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:10.370
public spending.

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.690
This strikes us as something very important.

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:19.060
I think France is one of these countries where
the level of spending relative to GDP is clearly

00:29:19.060 --> 00:29:20.630
too high.

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:27.390
And it has effects on the degree of taxation,
which is needed, and people are reacting worse

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:29.909
and worse to these very high taxes.

00:29:29.909 --> 00:29:36.220
So I think for France, it's clear that the
issue number one is to limit the size of the

00:29:36.220 --> 00:29:37.600
state, and eventually decrease it.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:40.160
So it's really a case-by-case answer.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:49.150
COHEN: What about the longer-term consequences
of this protracted period of high unemployment?

00:29:49.150 --> 00:30:03.860
BLANCHARD: So -- Larry Sum (ph) -- you know,
high unemployment was the major issue in Europe

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:05.809
in the 1980s.

00:30:05.809 --> 00:30:10.610
And so Larry Summers and I worked on that
issue at length at the time.

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:16.960
And we introduced in economics, existed in
physics, the word "hysteresis," which is the

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:21.299
notion that if you have a very long period
of unemployment, then some people are going

00:30:21.299 --> 00:30:25.000
to lose it, are going to lose the skills,
are going to lose the morale, are going to

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.910
lose what they need to do to become productive.

00:30:27.910 --> 00:30:33.250
And so long period of high unemployment leads
to a higher equilibrium level of unemployment

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:39.750
after that, because some people just are not
employable.

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:44.409
I worked on this for 10 years, and concluded
that the effect was there, but maybe was not

00:30:44.409 --> 00:30:45.590
dramatic.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:53.399
I concluded that what had happened in Europe
was institutional hysteresis, which is that

00:30:53.399 --> 00:30:59.309
in response to high unemployment, Europe had
introduced a number of labor market institutions,

00:30:59.309 --> 00:31:07.900
such as employment protection, which themselves
led to a higher level of unemployment; that

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:13.370
that was really the channel, it had introduced
labor market institutions, which were bad.

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:16.000
I have not seen this happen in this crisis.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:21.850
So I suspect that, for example, if you look
at long-term unemployment in the U.S., you

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:23.870
see that they can't get jobs.

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:25.340
And so you have to say, "Why?"

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:28.370
But one hypothesis is they are unemployable.

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:33.600
It's a bit unlikely that somebody who's been
out of work for nine months is unemployable.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:38.610
What happens -- and that's something on which
I worked with Peter Diamond at MIT -- is the

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:45.990
notion that when an employer receives 50 applications,
he has to rank them in some way.

00:31:45.990 --> 00:31:50.810
And he basically -- one of the criteria he's
going to use is how long have you been unemployed?

00:31:50.810 --> 00:31:55.039
So that in a market which is very depressed,
the long-term unemployed don't have a shot,

00:31:55.039 --> 00:31:57.120
at the back of the line.

00:31:57.120 --> 00:32:01.520
When the market becomes better, then, you
know, you have to choose between people, who

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:05.570
have been long-term unemployed, you don't
have a choice – there is nobody above them.

00:32:05.570 --> 00:32:08.130
The evidence is that they get the jobs.

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:09.960
Now, one very important thing.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:15.260
There is one reform which will not lead necessarily
to more growth, but would be highly desirable

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:21.340
in Europe which has to do with employment
protection.

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:26.990
And let me take two minutes, because I think
it's an interesting story.

00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:33.190
So when the '70s, the oil shocks came, and
unemployment increased, most governments in

00:32:33.190 --> 00:32:36.860
Europe tried to limit the amount of layoffs.

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:42.470
They put employment protection, high severance
pay, complex, traditional processes for which

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:43.470
you'd have to go.

00:32:43.470 --> 00:32:47.080
And if any of you has, you know, worked in
Europe, has had a firm in Europe, you know

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:49.850
how hard it is to get rid of people.

00:32:49.850 --> 00:32:56.179
It became clear in the '80s and the '90s,
that this was excessive, that it was just

00:32:56.179 --> 00:33:01.120
standing in the way of what's needed for an
economy to reallocate itself all the time.

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:07.720
So we had employment protection reforms, that
they took a very strange form.

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:11.460
They took the form of creating two types of
contracts.

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:17.779
So one was permanent contracts with extremely
high employment protection, and temporary

00:33:17.779 --> 00:33:21.510
contracts with little or no employment protection.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:24.919
So from the point of view of the firms, it
solved the problem.

00:33:24.919 --> 00:33:29.880
You basically keep a core group of workers,
and then you adjust with the others.

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:34.720
From the point of view of the union people,
who tend to be the ones with the permanent

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:40.310
contracts, it protects them, because they
have this set of people that are fired before

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:42.029
they are, or laid off before they are.

00:33:42.029 --> 00:33:44.019
But it's a catastrophe.

00:33:44.019 --> 00:33:50.480
It's a catastrophe, because the people who
are -- who have the temporary contracts are

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:51.480
the young.

00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:57.140
And so the lifecycle of the young at this
stage in Europe -- and this was already partly

00:33:57.140 --> 00:34:02.059
true before the crisis, but worse now -- is
you start with a temporary contract if you're

00:34:02.059 --> 00:34:03.059
lucky.

00:34:03.059 --> 00:34:07.010
Then you get unemployment for a while, then
you get another temporary contract, another

00:34:07.010 --> 00:34:08.840
one, so on and so on.

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:13.720
The firms which hire you do not feel any strong
incentive to train you, because they don't

00:34:13.720 --> 00:34:19.050
have the right to keep you for more than 12
or 18 months, unless they transform your contract

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:22.169
into a permanent one, which they don't want
to do.

00:34:22.169 --> 00:34:26.320
And so you have this dual labor market in
Europe, and this leads to unemployment rates

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:29.620
in Spain, as you know, for the younger, 50
percent.

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:32.340
Number's very similar in Italy.

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:37.470
That's a reform which has to happen, but you
can see how much resistance there is to it

00:34:37.470 --> 00:34:41.640
from the point -- from the point of view of
the people with the permanent contracts.

00:34:41.640 --> 00:34:45.089
So there's still progress being made, but
it is slow.

00:34:45.089 --> 00:34:49.450
I don't think it's going to lead to a burst
in growth, but it's going to lead to much

00:34:49.450 --> 00:34:52.970
fairer distribution of the burden.

00:34:52.970 --> 00:34:59.490
COHEN: One final question from me before we
turn it to our other attendees, and it has

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:07.260
to do with the engagement of central banks
around the world, and how they view their

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:08.260
roles.

00:35:08.260 --> 00:35:15.609
Clearly, the Federal Reserve was a standout
early in the global financial crisis, moving

00:35:15.609 --> 00:35:23.670
forward with monetary policy of a very accommodative
sort, but also in terms of its supervisory

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:32.010
and regulatory functions, imposing tough love
on the U.S. financial system in the form of

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:37.320
very significant meaningful stress tests,
and so on.

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:45.730
And one of the concerns has been that other
central banks are lagging in this regard.

00:35:45.730 --> 00:35:51.840
If you can give us an update, if you will,
in terms of the European Central Bank, the

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:59.079
way they view their role now, and also, touching
upon the change in structure there, where,

00:35:59.079 --> 00:36:05.490
unlike early in the financial crisis and the
severe recession, the ECB now does have some

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:08.990
regulatory authority on individual financial
institutions.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:18.490
BLANCHARD: So I think you're raising maybe
the number one issue in terms of the big issues,

00:36:18.490 --> 00:36:25.990
big policy issues we have to think about for
the next 10 years or more, which is one of

00:36:25.990 --> 00:36:36.940
the conclusions of the crisis is that you
cannot just control the economy with one rate,

00:36:36.940 --> 00:36:38.160
the policy rate.

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:44.970
There had been a convergence in academia,
and to some extent, in central banks, that

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:50.840
the right monetary policy was you have a policy
rate, you have a target, which is inflation,

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:55.210
you have a policy rate, and you move the policy
rates by 25 basis points, this way and that

00:36:55.210 --> 00:36:56.210
way.

00:36:56.210 --> 00:36:58.760
And if you're good enough, everything is fine.

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:02.820
And we learned that you can do this, and everything
looks fine at some margin, and it's going

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:07.380
very wrong at another other margin, then you
get the financial crisis we got.

00:37:07.380 --> 00:37:14.790
The conclusion is that we have to use macroprudential
tools, which is tools which are much more

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:15.790
specific.

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:20.010
If you're using it as a housing boom, you
don't use the policy rate, which affects the

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:24.560
whole economy, but you use the loan-to-value
ratio, a vice (ph) -- constraints on the capital

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:29.009
ratio associated with mortgages, for example.

00:37:29.009 --> 00:37:31.010
That's very nice.

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:32.849
It's right.

00:37:32.849 --> 00:37:44.180
It makes for an incredibly complex set of
arrangements, because basically the question

00:37:44.180 --> 00:37:47.619
is where do you put these -- these new tools?

00:37:47.619 --> 00:37:53.430
Conceptually, if you put them outside the
central bank, then you need coordination between

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:54.430
the two.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:55.430
It's very complex.

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:59.530
So most countries have concluded that has
to be within the central bank in some form.

00:37:59.530 --> 00:38:03.910
So, for example, in England, as you know,
there are two committees within the central

00:38:03.910 --> 00:38:06.640
bank; one monetary, and one financial.

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:14.090
And they interact with each other within the
bank.

00:38:14.090 --> 00:38:25.650
The issue is that the central banks -- is
now taking decisions which affect one group

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:29.920
or the other with very clear political implications.

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:35.410
Also, it is not entirely clear that the central
bank, which is independent, should be the

00:38:35.410 --> 00:38:40.300
place which decides how much capital goes
to this, how much capital goes to that.

00:38:40.300 --> 00:38:45.020
So both I think for conceptual and political
reasons, it's incredibly difficult to articulate

00:38:45.020 --> 00:38:49.450
exactly what the powers of the central bank
should be, what limitations.

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:54.510
How do you basically put some democratic ownership,
while not eliminating the independence of

00:38:54.510 --> 00:39:01.980
the central bank, and the central banks around
the world are struggling with this.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:04.420
You said the U.S. had done great.

00:39:04.420 --> 00:39:06.109
It did great earlier.

00:39:06.109 --> 00:39:10.680
But If I look now at the system of financial
regulation, the macroprudential tools, it's

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:12.760
a mess.

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:19.230
But, other countries are exploring their ways.

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:29.510
You took the example of the ECB, which has
been tasked with finding what the health of

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:34.930
banks is, and then forcing these banks to
take measures.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:38.100
This is an incredibly political job.

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:42.420
They are going to be under enormous pressure
here and there to do things.

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:46.990
And it's going to make for I think a very
complicated task.

00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:48.970
I think we haven't solved that issue.

00:39:48.970 --> 00:39:49.970
That issue is there.

00:39:49.970 --> 00:39:51.670
It's going to be there for a while.

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:53.350
COHEN: Thank you very much.

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:55.640
And now we'd like to open the floor.

00:39:55.640 --> 00:39:56.660
Yes, sir?

00:39:56.660 --> 00:40:01.260
QUESTION: Paul (ph) (inaudible) chief economist
at Standard &amp; Poor's.

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:03.610
Thank you very much for a very illuminating
talk and discussion.

00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:05.380
I'd like to focus back on Europe.

00:40:05.380 --> 00:40:09.460
I found it interesting that when you looked
at the Eurozone, you separated out the core

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:14.050
Europe, dealt with that, with the U.K., and
then came onto the periphery later.

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:20.280
But of course, the 17, now 18 countries in
the Eurozone are all in a single monetary

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:22.240
union.

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:25.640
And you touched on the issue of deflation
in the periphery.

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:30.440
But I wanted to sort of question you or get
your ideas about the architecture issue in

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:36.540
the Eurozone, and how you see that both as
an economist, a macroeconomist, and as a policymaker.

00:40:36.540 --> 00:40:38.349
Big development in 2012.

00:40:38.349 --> 00:40:43.349
Was the recognition at the highest level of
policymaking thinking in the -- being the

00:40:43.349 --> 00:40:48.630
Eurozone -- that the Eurozone architecture
was fundamentally flawed, and it needed to

00:40:48.630 --> 00:40:49.950
be fixed.

00:40:49.950 --> 00:40:55.190
And plans were set out to move, in their words,
towards a genuine economic and monetary union

00:40:55.190 --> 00:41:00.840
involving banking union, moves towards fiscal
union -- genuine economic integration, and

00:41:00.840 --> 00:41:02.140
political union.

00:41:02.140 --> 00:41:07.849
So seems to me that's one of the great sort
of issues of the day, which is going to really

00:41:07.849 --> 00:41:10.730
be important as it unfolds over the next few
years.

00:41:10.730 --> 00:41:14.640
I'd love to get your thoughts on -- as an
economist and policymaker, what you think

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:19.730
they should be doing in terms of the architecture,
and actually what Europe will look like, or

00:41:19.730 --> 00:41:22.250
the Eurozone in particular, will actually
look like.

00:41:22.250 --> 00:41:24.849
So perhaps the normative and a positive perspective?

00:41:24.849 --> 00:41:29.890
Thank you very much.

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:37.800
BLANCHARD: I think from normative point of
view, we all understand what's needed.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:43.210
So the banking union is clearly essential,
and there there is progress.

00:41:43.210 --> 00:41:46.470
I think it will happen.

00:41:46.470 --> 00:41:51.850
I think at least the initial exercise, which
is the so-called AQR, the stress test, will

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:52.960
take place.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:54.319
There'll be recapitalization.

00:41:54.319 --> 00:42:00.860
But as you know, we're not clear about the
-- how the -- where the money will come from,

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:01.960
if needed.

00:42:01.960 --> 00:42:06.920
But I think that's a process which -- which
will take place eventually.

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:16.910
On the fiscal union, I think -- I expect -- again,
from a normative point of view, it is quite

00:42:16.910 --> 00:42:18.770
-- it would be quite useful.

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:24.109
Let me, however, take a point here, that that's
not a panacea.

00:42:24.109 --> 00:42:31.460
Some people say that the reason why the U.S.
works as a common currency area is because

00:42:31.460 --> 00:42:35.760
of the fiscal structure, and the transfer
to the states.

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:36.790
That's playing some role.

00:42:36.790 --> 00:42:44.630
But for the U.S., what makes the U.S. function
as a currency area is mobility, which is that

00:42:44.630 --> 00:42:47.760
when a state is doing poorly, people leave
the state.

00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:52.400
When a state is doing well, people go to that
state.

00:42:52.400 --> 00:42:56.210
I did some work with Larry Katz many years
ago on that topic.

00:42:56.210 --> 00:42:58.550
And to me, it's the dominant factor.

00:42:58.550 --> 00:43:02.780
And that will never be the case in Europe.

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:05.119
So we'll never get to that point.

00:43:05.119 --> 00:43:12.680
Even if you have these two, I suspect that
these may not be enough to control, say, a

00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:17.440
housing boom in a country, or another, and
you may have to use macroprudential tools

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:18.440
as well.

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:20.480
And that's some of the issues I discussed
coming back in.

00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:24.770
So from a normative point of view, all these
things are needed.

00:43:24.770 --> 00:43:29.310
Even at the end, I'm not sure it would work
as well as the U.S., precisely for the reason

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:33.840
I have given, which is the lack of labor mobility.

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:38.630
It's -- even within countries, labor mobility's
much smaller than in the U.S., and across

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:41.619
borders, it is very limited.

00:43:41.619 --> 00:43:45.250
From a descriptive point of view, I suspect
that there'll be slow progress on the banking

00:43:45.250 --> 00:43:50.490
union, and very little else for the time being.

00:43:50.490 --> 00:43:55.550
QUESTION: Dick McCormick, CSIS.

00:43:55.550 --> 00:44:00.680
A couple of years ago, you were reported in
the media as favoring central banks, some

00:44:00.680 --> 00:44:05.230
central banks moving from a 2 percent inflation
target to a 4 percent inflation target.

00:44:05.230 --> 00:44:09.950
Are you still of this view, and if so, would
you give us the pluses and minuses of doing

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:11.329
this?

00:44:11.329 --> 00:44:16.810
BLANCHARD: Obviously, no minuses.

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:18.180
No.

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:22.009
I made -- so I think there are two arguments
here.

00:44:22.009 --> 00:44:23.310
I made the first one.

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:27.950
The question is how do I feel about the second
one.

00:44:27.950 --> 00:44:35.010
The argument I made is that when there's an
adverse shock hitting the economy, you want

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:40.599
to have the ability to decrease the interest
rate a lot, conceptually.

00:44:40.599 --> 00:44:44.710
How much you can do it depends on where you
are relative to zero.

00:44:44.710 --> 00:44:50.119
So I argued that if you want an economy at
2 percent inflation, and the real rate on

00:44:50.119 --> 00:44:55.700
average is 2 percent, then you're going to
typically start from 4 percent, 2 and 2, and

00:44:55.700 --> 00:44:56.880
you have a 4 percent margin.

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:01.080
Once you decrease your rate to zero, that's
what you can do.

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:03.650
Then you have to go to unconventional monetary
policy and so on.

00:45:03.650 --> 00:45:05.320
It doesn't work as well.

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:11.829
I argued that it might make sense to an economy
at 4 percent inflation, which will lead to

00:45:11.829 --> 00:45:14.440
an interest rate of 6 percent.

00:45:14.440 --> 00:45:19.420
And then you'd have 6 percent room to adjust
in case of an adverse shock.

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:29.280
I continue to think that if we'd had, say,
2 percent more to adjust the interest rate

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:36.150
in 2009 -- end of 2008, early 2009 -- it would
have helped.

00:45:36.150 --> 00:45:38.260
So it's kind of an insurance policy.

00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:41.670
There are going to be bad shocks once in a
while.

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:47.550
And you have a bit more margin if you operate
with, on average, a high interest rate.

00:45:47.550 --> 00:45:49.900
You're able to decrease the real rate.

00:45:49.900 --> 00:45:52.780
So that argument strikes me as right.

00:45:52.780 --> 00:45:58.210
The question is, well, what -- you know, it's
like you pay your insurance -- your life insurance

00:45:58.210 --> 00:45:59.210
policy.

00:45:59.210 --> 00:46:04.250
You pay it every month for some event which
you hope will not happen for a long time.

00:46:04.250 --> 00:46:05.579
Is it worth paying, right?

00:46:05.579 --> 00:46:08.290
Is it worth having 4 percent rather than 2
percent?

00:46:08.290 --> 00:46:13.069
And I argued that there's no solid evidence
anywhere in the world which shows that 4 percent

00:46:13.069 --> 00:46:17.609
is substantially more costly than 2 percent.

00:46:17.609 --> 00:46:22.690
The distortion's from inflation exist, but
they exist at higher rates of inflation.

00:46:22.690 --> 00:46:27.950
So seemed to me that it was a useful insurance
policy to buy, and that when we get back to

00:46:27.950 --> 00:46:32.200
some normal, I'd be happier to have it.

00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:36.820
There's a separate argument which has been
made recently, which is not with respect to

00:46:36.820 --> 00:46:41.510
macro, but with respect to fiscal, which is
we have a -- high debt levels.

00:46:41.510 --> 00:46:51.250
And either it's going to be repaid by taxpayers,
or maybe it can be decreased in value for

00:46:51.250 --> 00:46:52.250
inflation.

00:46:52.250 --> 00:46:56.810
So some people argue that one way of doing
it is basically to have an inflation rate

00:46:56.810 --> 00:47:02.869
which is higher, keep the nominal rate -- policy
rate low, so that in effect, the real rate

00:47:02.869 --> 00:47:03.869
is negative.

00:47:03.869 --> 00:47:08.300
And this allows you to decrease the debt level
more slowly.

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:11.910
But it allows you to decrease with that level
over time.

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:15.630
So this is using inflation to decrease the
debt level.

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:18.150
It seems to me that's a very dangerous route.

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:19.720
That's a very dangerous argument.

00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:25.680
I can see it in situations in which things
are so desperate that it's either this or

00:47:25.680 --> 00:47:30.059
default, in which case, you may want to have
a smooth default, relatively smooth default

00:47:30.059 --> 00:47:33.410
for inflation, rather than an explicit default.

00:47:33.410 --> 00:47:37.690
But I don't think that any country's anywhere
close to that.

00:47:37.690 --> 00:47:48.540
QUESTION: Byron William Blackstone (ph).

00:47:48.540 --> 00:47:54.480
You said that you expect China to grow at
about 7.5 percent.

00:47:54.480 --> 00:48:00.160
But at the same time, they're trying to rebalance
the economy away from infrastructure and state-owned

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:04.440
enterprise investment spending toward the
consumer.

00:48:04.440 --> 00:48:10.730
Now, my calculations show that there is no
way that they can accomplish that goal and

00:48:10.730 --> 00:48:12.730
grow at 7.5 percent.

00:48:12.730 --> 00:48:15.050
The growth has to slow.

00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:19.210
So how are your calculations different from
mine?

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:24.620
BLANCHARD: I would never question your calculations.

00:48:24.620 --> 00:48:30.550
I think that's a difference of horizons, which
is that this reallocation towards consumption

00:48:30.550 --> 00:48:32.119
is going to be very slow.

00:48:32.119 --> 00:48:37.980
So a 7.5 percent is for this year and next
year, in which this reallocation doesn't play

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:38.980
an essential role.

00:48:38.980 --> 00:48:47.440
If we look forward to the type of distribution
of production, which we think they are aiming

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:52.810
at, which is higher consumption and higher
consumption of services, then you're completely

00:48:52.810 --> 00:48:57.660
right in the sense that productivity growth,
at least measured productivity growth in services,

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:05.740
is lower than it is in manufacturing, so that
looking forward, say, five to 10 years, the

00:49:05.740 --> 00:49:14.410
range is much closer to 6 percent than to
7.5 percent.

00:49:14.410 --> 00:49:17.250
QUESTION: Thank you.

00:49:17.250 --> 00:49:19.430
Nise Sagwa of Pace University.

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:25.250
My question relates to the reactions to the
tapering, and it has two parts.

00:49:25.250 --> 00:49:31.089
The first is, do the markets, the financial
markets, react to flows or to stocks?

00:49:31.089 --> 00:49:34.660
And it seems to me that even the Fed is not
very clear on that.

00:49:34.660 --> 00:49:41.190
And the second is, is the effects of tapering
turn out to be very negative, will the Fed

00:49:41.190 --> 00:49:47.380
try to increase the money supply by encouraging
banks to lend out their reserves, either by

00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:54.089
having very low or negative interest rates
on reserve balances, or by some other measures?

00:49:54.089 --> 00:49:55.290
Thanks.

00:49:55.290 --> 00:50:03.369
BLANCHARD: So on the first, what do financial
markets react to, I think that's more a question

00:50:03.369 --> 00:50:05.980
that you should be answering rather than I.

00:50:05.980 --> 00:50:13.710
But I have a view, which is, you know, if
you look at what happened in May, I think

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:16.430
the reaction was not to a change in the flow.

00:50:16.430 --> 00:50:27.010
It was in a change in the perceived path of
first, less buying, and then selling of assets.

00:50:27.010 --> 00:50:31.480
And that was a reaction to, what I could call,
the "present value" of the whole path.

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:36.819
Basically, the markets decided that the Fed
was going to eventually slow down the rate

00:50:36.819 --> 00:50:40.230
of purchase, and then turn around and sell
it.

00:50:40.230 --> 00:50:45.349
So my sense is that the markets don't react
to flows – or to stocks.

00:50:45.349 --> 00:50:49.730
They react to what they perceive as the whole
strategy of the Fed.

00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:54.569
Now, clearly, when you decrease the flow,
you're giving an indication as to what you're

00:50:54.569 --> 00:50:56.140
going to do later.

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:58.350
And that affects the behavior of people.

00:50:58.350 --> 00:51:02.890
– In other words, it's not a mechanical
relation between putting $10 billion in the

00:51:02.890 --> 00:51:05.640
market and getting 10 basis points.

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:11.619
That's just -- you put 10, this is a signal
of a path, and this leads to markets to adjust

00:51:11.619 --> 00:51:13.240
the rate by 10 basis points.

00:51:13.240 --> 00:51:14.540
It's a way to think about it.

00:51:14.540 --> 00:51:18.280
I don't think it's an essential distinction.

00:51:18.280 --> 00:51:26.170
On what would happen if the tapering was negative,
well, they would slow it.

00:51:26.170 --> 00:51:32.329
And they really have no particular reason
to want to reduce their balance sheet extremely

00:51:32.329 --> 00:51:33.329
quickly.

00:51:33.329 --> 00:51:36.579
There's no obligation to reduce the balance
sheet quickly.

00:51:36.579 --> 00:51:39.190
So I think in this case, they would basically
keep the balance sheet.

00:51:39.190 --> 00:51:43.820
Now, the issue which would arise is if they
have to increase the interest rate at the

00:51:43.820 --> 00:51:48.119
time they still have a very large balance
sheet, then they would have to pay interest

00:51:48.119 --> 00:51:52.120
rate on the reserves in order to get people,
to get the banks to be willing to keep the

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:53.609
excess reserves.

00:51:53.609 --> 00:51:59.480
And that would -- would present a cost, from
an accounting point of view, for the Fed,

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:01.330
by paying interest.

00:52:01.330 --> 00:52:05.690
It would have, I think, from an economic point
of view, it's totally irrelevant.

00:52:05.690 --> 00:52:07.780
It's basically a check to treasury.

00:52:07.780 --> 00:52:09.990
We don't think that makes any difference.

00:52:09.990 --> 00:52:13.760
But from a political point of view, that would
look bad.

00:52:13.760 --> 00:52:21.869
If the Fed starts sending checks to banks,
I think that in the current political environment,

00:52:21.869 --> 00:52:26.480
Congress, some people in Congress, would react.

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:31.839
So I think the issue is political, but I don't
see this as a major one.

00:52:31.839 --> 00:52:34.690
(UNKNOWN): (OFF-MIKE)

00:52:34.690 --> 00:52:39.569
BLANCHARD: That would have to pay positive
in order to keep the banks willing to keep

00:52:39.569 --> 00:52:42.500
the reserves.

00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:45.440
QUESTION: Thank you.

00:52:45.440 --> 00:52:48.340
Carol Brookins (ph), former USCD, the World
Bank.

00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:52.750
I'd like to ask you about energy and this
drag on Europe's economies.

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:58.079
Energy costs in Europe are double, that of
the United States.

00:52:58.079 --> 00:53:03.640
Do you have any calculations on this impact
in terms of the drag on growth, particularly

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:07.380
in France, Germany, other countries?

00:53:07.380 --> 00:53:13.980
BLANCHARD: I will admit utter ignorance of
the issue.

00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:20.309
I do not know.

00:53:20.309 --> 00:53:24.109
COHEN: Yes, in the back, please?

00:53:24.109 --> 00:53:30.300
QUESTION: (Inaudible), U.S. companies have
posted a mixed picture in first quarter earnings

00:53:30.300 --> 00:53:32.890
so far.

00:53:32.890 --> 00:53:37.660
The earnings looks well, but the revenues,
not so good.

00:53:37.660 --> 00:53:40.980
So what's its implication for the economy?

00:53:40.980 --> 00:53:43.550
Thank you.

00:53:43.550 --> 00:53:48.069
BLANCHARD: Again, to me, maybe incorrectly,
these are -- these are bumps.

00:53:48.069 --> 00:53:52.800
These are not -- I mean, I think that U.S.
firms are doing well.

00:53:52.800 --> 00:53:54.609
Profits are large.

00:53:54.609 --> 00:54:02.931
One issue is why they are not investing more,
and I do not have a good explanation for that.

00:54:02.931 --> 00:54:05.820
But the kind of numbers you gave doesn't worry
me.

00:54:05.820 --> 00:54:12.210
COHEN: If I may interject, because these are
the sort of data that I do look at regularly.

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:19.650
I think it's much too early in this reporting
period to reach those sorts of conclusions.

00:54:19.650 --> 00:54:25.660
We still have many companies in the S&amp;P 500
left to report, point one.

00:54:25.660 --> 00:54:34.210
Point two, it would appear that Mr. Blanchard
has really touched on very important long-term

00:54:34.210 --> 00:54:39.829
issue, and that is U.S. companies have very
strong balance sheets; that is, the companies

00:54:39.829 --> 00:54:41.730
in the S&amp;P 500.

00:54:41.730 --> 00:54:44.230
And the question is, how are they using their
cash?

00:54:44.230 --> 00:54:49.600
And over the last few years, we've seen that
more than usual, about 10 percentage points

00:54:49.600 --> 00:54:55.880
more than usual, have gone for things like
dividend increases and share purchases, and

00:54:55.880 --> 00:55:01.930
that 10 percent has come out of things like
long-term investment and investment in research.

00:55:01.930 --> 00:55:05.260
And that really is, I think, the long-term
question.

00:55:05.260 --> 00:55:10.340
By the way, there is -- there are comparable
data points for the federal government.

00:55:10.340 --> 00:55:17.319
The federal government is also investing much
less than it usually has invested in terms

00:55:17.319 --> 00:55:21.620
of infrastructure, but also in terms of basic
research.

00:55:21.620 --> 00:55:28.860
Just by way of example, the U.S. government
used to invest 4.5 percent of GDP in things

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:30.600
like basic research and R&amp;D.

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:33.590
We're now down to 2.5 percent.

00:55:33.590 --> 00:55:36.140
That is not what it needs to be, in my view.

00:55:36.140 --> 00:55:43.490
BLANCHARD: But the reasons for public and
private investment being low has been different.

00:55:43.490 --> 00:55:49.700
What is your main guess as to why firms are
reluctant to do long-term investment?

00:55:49.700 --> 00:55:55.530
Is it -- is it uncertainty of some sort, about
how the world looks like in five years?

00:55:55.530 --> 00:55:56.950
Or is it something else?

00:55:56.950 --> 00:56:02.470
COHEN: There are many theories out there,
some people talking about uncertainty.

00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:08.320
I think the number one reason is the output
gap with capacity utilization rates in the

00:56:08.320 --> 00:56:15.540
United States finally -- finally moving up
to the point where new investment would be

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:16.540
encouraged.

00:56:16.540 --> 00:56:21.850
The other thing that we are watching very
carefully, and the data look better over the

00:56:21.850 --> 00:56:27.740
last few months, is the creation of new jobs
in the private sector.

00:56:27.740 --> 00:56:31.540
We always look at that headline number in
terms of jobs created.

00:56:31.540 --> 00:56:37.549
Let's keep in mind the public sector has cut
back 600,000 or 700,000 jobs.

00:56:37.549 --> 00:56:42.750
But there is now much better job creation
taking place in the private sector in the

00:56:42.750 --> 00:56:43.750
United States.

00:56:43.750 --> 00:56:49.369
I think we have time for one more quick question.

00:56:49.369 --> 00:56:51.340
I think we're good.

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:57.971
I would like to thank Monsieur Blanchard for
being here on behalf of all of us, and for

00:56:57.971 --> 00:56:59.220
a wonderful discussion this morning.

00:56:59.220 --> 00:57:00.220
BLANCHARD: Thank you very much.

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:01.220
(APPLAUSE)

00:57:01.220 --> 00:57:01.221
END

